; ======================================================================
; neon.pi4 - THE NEONARCADE DRAWING ENGINE, RASTERISED BY V3D.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; This is the Pi 4 port of the chrome kit in
;  the engine's chrome module (GlUIChrome, in the OpenGL engine project)
; and of the two drawing primitives it lowers to in GlUtilities.pbi.
; Every pixel it puts on the screen is put there by the V3D 4.2 GPU.
;
; THERE IS NO CPU BACKEND IN THIS FILE AND THERE IS NO SEAM FOR ONE.
; That is a decision, not an omission, and the number behind it is
; measured on this board: a full-surface alpha blend costs the A72
; 2172.0 ms against a 25 ms frame budget - 87x over, about 1815 cycles
; per blended pixel (pi4NeonLiar.pi4:256-262). Every glyph and every
; Neon_Glow ring in the chrome kit is a blend. On V3D the blend is
; fixed-function in the tile buffer - SRC_ALPHA = 6, INV_SRC_ALPHA = 7
; in packet 84, v3d_packet.xml:21-22 and :678-686 - and the overdraw
; never reaches DRAM at all.
;
; ======================================================================
; USE - in the MAIN file, in this order. Libraries do not include
; libraries (project ruling, 2026-07-30, house rule 10), so the main
; file is what orders them:
;
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4"
;     #DSP_DMA_LINKED = 0
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/display.pi4"
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/v3dqpu.pi4"
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/v3d.pi4"
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/neon.pi4"
;
; display.pi4 is here for the SURFACE ONLY - DisplayInit(), DisplayBase(),
; DisplayWidth/Height/Pitch(). Not one pixel in this file is drawn by it.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE FOUR OPERATIONS, AND WHY THERE ARE ONLY FOUR
; ======================================================================
; GlUIChrome.pbi is 1367 lines and every widget in it composes down to
; two primitives plus a clip rectangle:
;
;   Neon_Box        GlUIChrome.pbi:302-304  -> DrawGLBox, 6 verts,
;                                              GL_TRIANGLES, one draw
;                                              call per rectangle
;                                              (GlUtilities.pbi:1998-2027)
;   Neon_Text       GlUIChrome.pbi:306-308  -> DrawGLText, tinted quads
;                                              (GlUtilities.pbi:1803-1930)
;   Neon_Scissor*   GlUIChrome.pbi:404-422  -> glScissor + enable
;
; Neon_Frame is four boxes (:330-335). Neon_Glow is four FILLED boxes,
; innermost first, at alpha strength/(i*1.5) for i = 1..4 (:323-328) -
; they are boxes and not rings, and the draw order is load bearing.
; Neon_Panel is five boxes and a text (:339-348). The menu check mark
; is seven boxes (:864-886). This file transcribes those compositions
; literally.
;
; Outside the IDE the engine also draws a convex fan and a line list
; (GlUtilities.pbi:1955-1989 and :2030-2052). Both are the same vertex
; path with a different primitive mode, so both are here:
; Neon_Fan and Neon_Lines.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE COORDINATE SYSTEM, AND THE TWO PLACES IT IS EASY TO GET WRONG
; ======================================================================
; GlUtilities.pbi:2010-2011 does
;
;     nx = (X / TW) * 2 - 1        ny = 1 - (Y / TH) * 2
;
; so the engine's origin is the TOP LEFT of the canvas, +X is right and
; +Y IS DOWN. In #GL_VIEWPORT_NATIVE, which is the mode the IDE host
; runs, one canvas unit is exactly one physical pixel
; (GlUtilities.pbi:2233-2247), so every coordinate in this file is a
; pixel and there is no letterbox arithmetic anywhere.
;
; V3D's shaded vertex is not in pixels. Reference/mesa-24.3.4_
; v3d_nir_lower_io.c:492-560: Xs and Ys are INTEGERS in 1/256ths of a
; pixel, RELATIVE TO THE VIEWPORT CENTRE, and the shader adds no offset
; - VIEWPORT_OFFSET carries the centre. So
;
;     Xs = (x - centreX) * 256        Ys = (y - centreY) * 256
;
; and the attribute records for those two slots declare INT, SIGNED,
; READ AS INT. A vertex 24 pixels right of centre is $00001800, which
; READ AS A FLOAT IS A DENORMAL; an attribute declared float would be
; flushed to zero and collapse every primitive onto the viewport
; centre, which looks exactly like a wrong transform.
;
; Y DIRECTION IS NOT GUESSED. pi4V3dTri.pi4 counted both conventions on
; silicon and the board said: Y INCREASES WITH THE STORED ROW. So the
; engine's +Y-down and V3D's +Ys agree and no flip is needed. That is a
; measurement, 2026-08-28, and it is the only reason this file does not
; flip.
;
; ======================================================================
; COLOUR IS A PACKED INTEGER AND THERE IS NO FLOATING POINT ANYWHERE
; ======================================================================
; The engine's colours are .f triples in 0..1. This backend refuses .f
; outright (EmitCore_A64.pbi:543-544) and a software binary32 multiply
; costs 1751 instructions (math.pi4:114-125). So the public API takes
;
;     colour = (a << 24) | (r << 16) | (g << 8) | b,  each 0..255
;
; built by Neon_RGB / Neon_RGBA, and the palette's derived products -
; accent * 0.30, accent * 0.55, accent * 0.8 - are integer ratios
; through Neon_Mul(). The ONLY float in the file is the IEEE 754 bit
; pattern the GPU needs, and neon_F32Ratio() builds those bit patterns
; with integer long division. It is exact where the ratio is exact and
; correctly rounded where it is not; see its own comment.
;
; ======================================================================
; ONE DRAW CALL PER RECTANGLE, WHICH IS WHAT THE ENGINE DOES TOO
; ======================================================================
; GlUtilities.pbi:2024-2026 issues one glDrawArrays and one buffer
; upload per box. This file does the same, for a reason that is
; V3D's rather than the engine's: the colour reaches the fragment
; shader through the UNIFORM STREAM, and the uniform address lives in
; the GL Shader State Record. Two rectangles of different colours are
; therefore two records and two GL_SHADER_STATE packets.
;
; What is NOT duplicated is the vertex buffer. Every primitive in a
; frame writes into one array and the draw names its slice with
; VERTEX_ARRAY_PRIMS's Index Of First Vertex field (v3d_packet.xml:508-513),
; so the four attribute records in each shader record all point at the
; same base and only the first-vertex index moves.
;
; A TEXT RUN IS ONE DRAW CALL, NOT ONE PER GLYPH. Every glyph in a
; string is the same colour, so the whole string is decomposed into
; rectangles, all of them appended to the vertex array, and issued as a
; single VERTEX_ARRAY_PRIMS. That is the same batching decision
; GlUtilities.pbi:1879 makes at 256 quads per call, arrived at from the
; other direction.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT IS PROVEN AND WHAT IS NOT - keep this honest
; ======================================================================
; Proven on silicon before this file existed:
;   * the binner and the renderer, 4096 pixels of a clear counted
;     (V3D stage 3, 2026-08-28)
;   * a rasterised triangle, 1128 pixels counted, three identical runs,
;     with coordinate, vertex and fragment shaders (V3D stage 4)
;   * the LDVPM family, the shader state record and the attribute
;     record encoders
;
; What this file adds and what therefore has to be proven by ITS OWN
; counted-pixel runs: multi-tile rendering, a page table that reaches
; the framebuffer, fixed-function blending, more than one draw call in
; a list, the clip window, and the fan and line primitive modes.
; ======================================================================


; ======================================================================
;  ERRORS
;
;  Loud refusals, never a silently wrong picture. Every one of these is
;  returned AND latched in Neon_Error() so a caller that ignores a
;  return value still cannot get a clean-looking frame out of a broken
;  one.
; ======================================================================
#NEON_OK            = 0
#NEON_ERR_ARENA     = 1    ; the arena is missing, misaligned or too small
#NEON_ERR_SURFACE   = 2    ; no surface bound, or not 32 bits per pixel
#NEON_ERR_V3D       = 3    ; V3dInit() or the MMU refused - V3dError() says
#NEON_ERR_GEOMETRY  = 4    ; V3dRenderBegin() refused the surface size
#NEON_ERR_ONETILE   = 5    ; the surface is a single tile - see NeonInit()
#NEON_ERR_SHADER    = 6    ; a QPU program would not encode
#NEON_ERR_VERTS     = 7    ; the vertex arena is full
#NEON_ERR_RECORDS   = 8    ; the shader-record arena is full
#NEON_ERR_LIST      = 9    ; the bin list is full
#NEON_ERR_NOFRAME   = 10   ; a primitive outside NeonFrameBegin/End
#NEON_ERR_INFRAME   = 11   ; NeonFrameBegin() inside a frame
#NEON_ERR_BIN       = 12   ; the binner did not finish
#NEON_ERR_RENDER    = 13   ; the renderer did not finish
#NEON_ERR_FONT      = 14   ; no font bound
#NEON_ERR_ARGS      = 15   ; a parameter out of range
#NEON_ERR_BACKEND   = 16   ; selected renderer lifecycle refused

#NEON_BACKEND_NATIVE   = 0
#NEON_BACKEND_EXTERNAL = 1

; ======================================================================
;  LIMITS
;
;  These size the arena and nothing else; they are checked, and going
;  past one is a refusal and not a wrap. #NEON_MAX_DRAWS is the number
;  of DRAW CALLS - boxes plus text runs plus fans plus line lists - and
;  #NEON_MAX_VERTS the number of vertices behind them.
;
;  The AVR emulator panel, which is the heaviest real frame this engine
;  has ever drawn, measured 3505 shape draws, 331 text runs and 1434
;  glyphs (_GlDrawBench.pb:14-18). 4096 draws and 131072 vertices cover
;  it with the text decomposed into rectangles.
; ======================================================================
#NEON_MAX_DRAWS   = 4096
#NEON_MAX_VERTS   = 262144

#NEON_VERT_BYTES  = 16     ; Xc Yc Xs Ys, four 32-bit values
#NEON_ATTRS       = 4      ; and therefore four attribute records
#NEON_SHREC_STRIDE = 128   ; 36 record + 4 * 16 attrs = 100, rounded up
                           ; to keep every record 32-byte aligned, which
                           ; V3dShaderRecordBegin() requires (v3d.pi4:5490)
#NEON_UNIF_STRIDE = 32     ; R G B A and the TLB config word, rounded up

; The uniform word a write to TLBU pulls out of the stream by itself.
; nir_to_vir.c:1817-1820: the specifier register is "preloaded with
; 0xffffffff (0xff gets you normal vec4 f16 RT0 writes)". Written
; explicitly rather than relied upon, exactly as Mesa writes it.
#NEON_TLB_CONF = $FFFFFFFF

; f32 bit patterns this file needs as constants.
#NEON_F_ZERO = $00000000
#NEON_F_ONE  = $3F800000
#NEON_F_MONE = $BF800000

; The QPU code slot. Three programs, 512 bytes each, which is what
; pi4V3dTri.pi4 used and is far more than any of them needs.
#NEON_CODE_SLOT = 512

; Primitive modes, from v3d.pi4's own enum, restated here so a caller
; of Neon_Fan/Neon_Lines does not have to include the V3D names.
#NEON_PRIM_TRIS     = 4
#NEON_PRIM_FAN      = 6
#NEON_PRIM_LINE     = 1
#NEON_PRIM_LINELOOP = 2

; ======================================================================
;  THE TEXTURED PATH (M3) - ONE QUAD PER GLYPH, SAMPLED FROM AN ATLAS
;
;  This is a SECOND, PARALLEL pipeline that lives alongside the flat one
;  above. It exists so text can draw as one textured quad per glyph
;  instead of the ~7 alpha-blended rectangles neon_GlyphRects emits.
;
;  WHY A PARALLEL VERTEX BUFFER AND NOT A WIDER ONE. The flat vertex is
;  16 bytes - Xc Yc Xs Ys - and neon_Rect writes it in the hot loop of
;  the whole engine (its own comment: 53,710 vertices, 407 ms of a
;  661 ms frame). The textured vertex is pi4V3dTex's proven 40-byte,
;  ten-attribute layout - Xc Yc Zc Wc Xs Ys Zs 1/Wc U V - carrying the
;  two texture-coordinate varyings. Widening the flat vertex to 40
;  bytes would bloat every rectangle 2.5x in memory AND force the flat
;  coordinate/vertex shaders (which synthesise Zc Wc Zs 1/Wc from
;  constants and read only four attributes) to change, breaking the
;  shape M1/M2 proved on silicon. So the textured path gets its OWN
;  vertex array, its OWN shader records and its OWN three QPU programs,
;  and the flat chrome kit is untouched. The two draw kinds interleave
;  freely in one bin list: each carries its own record address and its
;  own VERTEX_ARRAY_PRIMS first-index into its own array.
; ======================================================================
#NEON_TVERT_BYTES   = 40    ; Xc Yc Zc Wc Xs Ys Zs 1/Wc U V, ten 32-bit
#NEON_TATTRS        = 10
#NEON_TSHREC_STRIDE = 256   ; 36 record + 10 * 16 attrs = 196, rounded up
                            ; to a 32-byte-aligned 256
#NEON_MAX_TDRAWS    = 2048  ; textured DRAW CALLS - one per Neon_TextTex
#NEON_MAX_TVERTS    = 98304 ; 6 verts a glyph -> ~16k glyphs, a full screen

; The atlas is FIXED WIDTH 16. The TMU derives the UBLINEAR_2_COLUMN
; tiling from an image width of 16 (pi4V3dAtlas's whole M2 lesson), so
; the width must not change without recomputing Ub2Off. Height is
; padded to a power of two so V = vTexel / height is exact. The carved
; buffers are sized for the maximum; the TFU tiles only the height the
; current font actually needs.
#NEON_ATLAS_W       = 16
#NEON_ATLAS_MAXH    = 4096
#NEON_ATLAS_MAXBYTES = 262144   ; 16 * 4096 * 4

; A caller may copy this value out through Neon_AtlasRasterGlyphUV().
; It never receives neon_glyphUV itself: the renderer keeps ownership of
; that table, just as it keeps ownership of the raster pixels.
Structure NeonAtlasGlyphUV
  left.i
  right.i
  top.i
  bottom.i
EndStructure

; How the textured fragment shader turns atlas coverage into colour.
;   1 (default) - MODULATE: out = (colour.rgb, texel.a * colour.a). One
;                 FMUL on the mul pipe, honouring the caller's alpha
;                 exactly as the flat path does. This is the M3 goal.
;   0           - COVERAGE ONLY: out = (colour.rgb, texel.a). No FMUL,
;                 so nothing on the textured path uses an op the M1/M2
;                 shaders did not; colour.alpha is ignored (text draws
;                 at the glyph's own coverage). The safe fallback if the
;                 FMUL ever proves wrong on silicon - rebuild with this
;                 set to 0, nothing else changes.
#NEON_TEXTEX_MODULATE = 1

; ======================================================================
;  STATE
;
;  One name per Global - this dialect takes one declaration per line.
;  No procedure here is re-entrant and none recurses: parameters and
;  locals are static .bss slots on this backend.
; ======================================================================
Global neon_err.i      = #NEON_OK
Global gNeonReady.i    = 0        ; NeonInit() completed
Global neon_inFrame.i  = 0

; ---- optional primitive backend -------------------------------------
; The widget layer below is intentionally renderer-neutral.  Normally its
; boxes, text, scissors, fans and lines fall through to Neon's native V3D
; frame owner.
; A resident renderer such as the Vulkan chrome adapter can install these
; entry points and reuse every existing primitive unchanged without
; entering NeonFrameBegin/NeonFrameEnd recursively.  The hook signatures
; exactly match the public Neon primitive procedures.
; Ownership stays with the installer; Neon only borrows the addresses.
Global gNeonBackendBox.i          = 0
Global gNeonBackendText.i         = 0
Global gNeonBackendScissorSet.i   = 0
Global gNeonBackendScissorClear.i = 0
Global gNeonBackendFanBegin.i     = 0
Global gNeonBackendFanPoint.i     = 0
Global gNeonBackendFanEnd.i       = 0
Global gNeonBackendFanOutline.i   = 0
Global gNeonBackendLinesBegin.i   = 0
Global gNeonBackendLine.i         = 0
Global gNeonBackendLinesEnd.i     = 0
Global gNeonBackendActive.i       = 0
; Optional renderer measurements pair with the draw backend. A negative
; callback result selects the native bitmap measurement path.
Global gNeonMeasureWidth.i        = 0
Global gNeonMeasureLineHeight.i   = 0
Global gNeonMeasureActive.i       = 0

; Renderer-neutral lifecycle seam. Native V3D remains the default. An
; external renderer installs all four callbacks atomically and then owns
; the public lifecycle without calling these public procedures recursively.
Global gNeonLifecycleMode.i       = #NEON_BACKEND_NATIVE
Declare.i neon_LifecycleInitUnbound()
Declare.i neon_LifecycleBeginUnbound(colour.i)
Declare.i neon_LifecycleEndUnbound()
Declare.i neon_LifecycleShutdownUnbound()
Global gNeonLifecycleInit.i       = @neon_LifecycleInitUnbound
Global gNeonLifecycleBegin.i      = @neon_LifecycleBeginUnbound
Global gNeonLifecycleEnd.i        = @neon_LifecycleEndUnbound
Global gNeonLifecycleShutdown.i   = @neon_LifecycleShutdownUnbound
Global gNeonLifecycleError.i      = 0

; ---- the arena -------------------------------------------------------
Global neon_arena.i    = 0
Global neon_arenaEnd.i = 0
Global neon_need.i     = 0        ; bytes NeonInit() worked out it needs

; ---- the surface -----------------------------------------------------
Global neon_fb.i       = 0        ; ARM physical address of pixel (0,0)
Global neon_w.i        = 0        ; logical canvas width (layout / hit testing)
Global neon_h.i        = 0        ; logical canvas height
Global neon_physW.i    = 0        ; raster target width after rotation
Global neon_physH.i    = 0        ; raster target height after rotation
Global neon_rot.i      = 0        ; logical-to-raster rotation, 0/90/180/270
Global neon_pitch.i    = 0
Global neon_fbBytes.i  = 0        ; pitch * height (one screen)
Global neon_fbMapBytes.i = 0      ; framebuffer bytes to MAP in the V3D page
                                  ; table - >= neon_fbBytes. Larger than one
                                  ; screen when a double-height surface is in
                                  ; use, so BOTH halves are mapped and the
                                  ; render target can be flipped between them
                                  ; without rebuilding the page table.
Global neon_mapBase.i   = 0       ; immutable mapped-span base after init
Global neon_mapBytes.i  = 0       ; immutable mapped-span extent after init
Global neon_extraMapBase.i = 0     ; optional separate identity-mapped GPU heap
Global neon_extraMapBytes.i = 0
Global neon_capPhysW.i  = 0       ; largest physical geometry pools can hold
Global neon_capPhysH.i  = 0
Global neon_outFmt.i    = #V3D_OFMT_RGBA8
Global neon_cx.i       = 0        ; physical viewport centre, pixels
Global neon_cy.i       = 0

; ---- the carved buffers ---------------------------------------------
Global neon_pt.i       = 0        ; V3D page table
Global gNeonPtBytes.i  = 0
Global neon_illegal.i  = 0        ; the page every unmapped PTE points at
Global neon_code.i     = 0        ; three QPU programs
Global neon_codeBytes.i = 0
Global neon_unifCs.i   = 0        ; the coordinate shader's uniforms
Global neon_unifVs.i   = 0        ; the vertex shader's
Global neon_tstate.i   = 0
Global neon_tstateBytes.i = 0
Global neon_talloc.i   = 0
Global neon_tallocBytes.i = 0
Global neon_overflow.i = 0
Global neon_overflowBytes.i = 0
Global neon_bcl.i      = 0
Global gNeonBclBytes.i = 0
Global neon_tilelist.i = 0
Global neon_tilelistBytes.i = 0
Global neon_rcl.i      = 0
Global neon_rclBytes.i = 0
Global neon_shrec.i    = 0        ; #NEON_MAX_DRAWS records
Global neon_shrecBytes.i = 0
Global neon_unif.i     = 0        ; #NEON_MAX_DRAWS fragment uniform blocks
Global neon_unifBytes.i = 0
Global gNeonVerts.i    = 0
Global neon_vertsBytes.i = 0
Global neon_guard.i    = 0        ; DELIBERATELY NOT MAPPED. See NeonInit().
Global neon_top.i      = 0        ; one past everything above

; ---- per-frame cursors ----------------------------------------------
Global neon_nDraws.i   = 0
Global neon_nVerts.i   = 0
Global neon_nBoxes.i   = 0        ; counted separately, for the report
Global neon_nGlyphs.i  = 0
Global neon_nRuns.i    = 0
Global neon_nClipped.i = 0        ; primitives the clip rejected outright

; ---- where a frame's microseconds went -------------------------------
;  Four numbers, because "the frame took 265 ms" names nothing. The
;  A72 building lists and the V3D rasterising them are different
;  problems with different fixes, and the clean is a third.
Global gNeonUsBegin.i  = 0        ; V3dRclBuild plus the bin prologue
Global gNeonUsClean.i  = 0        ; the cache maintenance before submit
Global gNeonUsBin.i    = 0        ; submit and wait, the binner
Global gNeonUsRender.i = 0        ; submit and wait, the renderer

; ---- the clip rectangle ---------------------------------------------
;  Neon_ScissorSet lowers to V3D's CLIP_WINDOW packet, which is
;  hardware and pixel-exact. The values are also kept here so the
;  CPU-side cull below can drop a primitive that cannot contribute
;  before it costs a shader record.
Global neon_clipOn.i   = 0
Global neon_clipX.i    = 0
Global neon_clipY.i    = 0
Global neon_clipW.i    = 0
Global neon_clipH.i    = 0

; ---- the font --------------------------------------------------------
Global neon_fbits.i    = 0        ; address of the 1bpp glyph blob
Global neon_fw.i       = 0        ; cell width, pixels
Global neon_fh.i       = 0        ; cell height
Global neon_frb.i      = 0        ; bytes per glyph row
Global neon_ffirst.i   = 0
Global neon_flast.i    = 0

; Four font slots, matching the engine's four faces (GlUIChrome.pbi:73-76).
; The engine's are separate rasterised atlases at different point sizes;
; here they are the same 1bpp cell at integer scales, which is what a
; 1-bit font can honestly do. Index 0 UI, 1 small, 2 mono, 3 bold.
Global Dim neon_fontScale.i[4]
Global Dim neon_fontEmbolden.i[4]

; ---- the textured path's carved buffers (see the M3 header) ----------
Global neon_texSrc.i    = 0       ; raster RGBA8 atlas, the TFU reads it
Global neon_tex.i       = 0       ; UBLINEAR_2 atlas, TFU writes / TMU reads
Global neon_texState.i  = 0       ; texture state +0, sampler state +256
Global neon_unifTcs.i   = 0       ; textured coord shader's constant uniforms
Global neon_unifTvs.i   = 0       ; textured vertex shader's
Global neon_tshrec.i    = 0       ; #NEON_MAX_TDRAWS textured records
Global neon_tshrecBytes.i = 0
Global neon_tunif.i     = 0       ; #NEON_MAX_TDRAWS textured FS uniform blocks
Global neon_tunifBytes.i = 0
Global neon_tverts.i    = 0
Global neon_tvertsBytes.i = 0
Global neon_glyphUV.i   = 0       ; per-glyph cell-edge UVs, CPU-only (unmapped)

; ---- the textured path's per-frame cursors ---------------------------
Global neon_nTDraws.i   = 0
Global neon_nTVerts.i   = 0
Global neon_nTGlyphs.i  = 0
; What the bin list currently says the non-perspective flags are, so a
; run of textured draws costs one NON_PERSPECTIVE_FLAGS packet, not one
; per glyph string. The prologue zeros the flags, so 0 is the reset.
Global neon_emitNP.i    = 0

; ---- the atlas, once built (lazily, from the current font) ------------
Global neon_texBuilt.i    = 0     ; the atlas has been filled and tiled
Global neon_texFontBits.i = 0     ; the font it was built for; a change rebuilds
Global neon_rasterBuilt.i    = 0  ; CPU RGBA atlas and UV table are complete
Global neon_rasterFontBits.i = 0  ; font which owns that immutable snapshot
Global neon_rasterW.i        = 0  ; dimensions of the borrowed linear view
Global neon_rasterH.i        = 0
Global neon_rasterBytes.i    = 0  ; exact populated CPU raster extent
Global gNeonAtlasW.i      = 0     ; always #NEON_ATLAS_W
Global gNeonAtlasH.i      = 0     ; padded to a power of two
Global neon_atlasCols.i   = 0     ; #NEON_ATLAS_W / cell width
Global neon_atlasGlyphs.i = 0     ; last - first + 1

; ======================================================================
;  THREE LOOKUP TABLES, AND WHY THEY ARE NOT AN OPTIMISATION
;
;  neon_F32Ratio is integer long division with a normalise and a round.
;  It is correct and it is not fast, and TWO OF THEM ARE ON THE
;  PER-VERTEX PATH - the clip coordinates Xc and Yc. A chrome frame
;  with two thousand glyphs in it decomposes into something like a
;  hundred thousand vertices, and with the ARM MMU off every
;  instruction fetch is Device memory. Two divisions a vertex there is
;  not a slow frame, it is a frame that does not finish inside the
;  15 s deadman.
;
;  Every clip coordinate this engine can produce is (x - cx) / cx for
;  an INTEGER x, so there is a finite set of them and it is small. The
;  tables hold that set: x from -w to 2w and y from -h to 2h, which
;  covers a rectangle placed a whole screen off any edge. Outside that
;  range - which nothing in the chrome kit does - neon_Vertex falls
;  back to the exact routine, so the table is a cache and never a
;  restriction.
;
;  neon_F32Byte is the third: 256 possible values, four of them per
;  draw call.
; ======================================================================
Global neon_xcTab.i = 0           ; 3 * width  entries, biased by width
Global neon_ycTab.i = 0           ; 3 * height entries, biased by height
Global Dim neon_byteF32.i[256]

; ======================================================================
;  ACCESSORS
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i Neon_Error()
  ProcedureReturn neon_err
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Neon_Ready()
  ProcedureReturn gNeonReady
EndProcedure

; These private, never-selected targets give the compiler an exact
; signature for each indirect-call slot even in programs which use only the
; native renderer.  The active flag, not a magic null target, selects a
; backend.  Real targets arrive through NeonDrawBackendInstall below.
Procedure.i neon_BackendBoxUnbound(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i, colour.i)
  ProcedureReturn #NEON_ERR_NOFRAME
EndProcedure

Procedure.i neon_BackendTextUnbound(font.i, x.i, y.i, *s, colour.i)
  ProcedureReturn #NEON_ERR_NOFRAME
EndProcedure

Procedure.i neon_MeasureWidthUnbound(font.i, *s)
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i neon_MeasureLineHeightUnbound(font.i)
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

